listings.desc:
As a Working Student in Verification & Validation & Test Automation you will have the opportunity to work with top verification and validation engineers and understand how automotive systems work. 
 In our  Working Student program you will learn:
 how the automotive systems work;
 how to run tests in the Hardware-in-the-Loop environment;
 communication protocols used in automotive;
 testing principles and development of test cases;
 automation of test cases using Python and internal tools;
 analysis of failures, bugs.
 Technology stack: 
 Python;
 C++;
 Computer Vision Basics;
 AI / Machine Learning Fundamentals;
 Linux Basics;
 Version source control (e.g. Git, Bitbucket);
 Communication protocols (e.g. Ethernet, CAN, Flexray, SPI, I2C);
 Vehicle Simulation Tools;
 Software in the Loop;
 Hardware in the Loop;
 Hardware (Oscilloscopes, programmable power supplies, PCBs, debuggers).
 Testing Fundamentals (Test Process, Test methods);
 Programming;
 Automotive / ADAS Awareness;
 Microcontrollers/Microprocessors;
 Electronics;
 Physics;
 Data Annotation Experience.
 The languages you should know: 
 English – a good level is mandatory.

Your contribution to something big: 
 Support the design, implementation, and maintenance of cloud and infrastructure solutions;
 Assist with Infrastructure-as-Code (IaC) development using Terraform;
 Contribute to CI/CD pipelines and DevOps automation activities;
 Help monitor, troubleshoot, and optimize platform infrastructure;
 Collaborate with development teams to improve deployment and operational processes;
 Optionally contribute to frontend/UI development activities, depending on skills and interests.
 What distinguishes you:  
 Currently pursuing a degree in Computer Science, Software Engineering, IT, or a related field;
 Knowledge of DevOps concepts and software development lifecycle principles;
 Familiarity with Git and CI/CD concepts is mandatory;
 Good communication skills in English & Portuguese;
 Strong analytical, problem-solving, and communication skills.

